Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Infra Substrates and Observers", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Reviewed by Pavel Hajek on 27 October 2026.", "TI"]], \
"GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We consider discrete substrates given by a ",
 StyleBox["graph",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". A hypothetical ",
 StyleBox["infra geometer",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " walks on the graph, organizes his observations and builds a notion of ",
 StyleBox["graph geometry",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". In general, we can imagine a ",
 StyleBox["computational observer",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " with bounded computation time and memory, and ask how it perceives the \
graph."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The substrate we consider here would be called a ",
 StyleBox["spatial graph",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " in the Wolfram Physics Project. Its vertices correspond to events in a ",
 StyleBox["simultaneity slice",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " of the causal graph of a single rewriting history, for a chosen observer. ",
 StyleBox["Spatial connectivity",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " comes from the lowest common ancestor relation, connecting vertices just \
one light tick away, while ",
 StyleBox["persistence",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " across time comes from forward causal relations. This is how space emerges \
in the hypergraph rewriting model for a particular observer."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "As a minimal model of dependence on the observer, we introduce the scalar \
",
 StyleBox["observer scale",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "f2acf1fd-e16b-4762-b354-3ae53d969390"],
 ". The observer aggregates his measurements at this scale. In concrete \
cases, we can think of him using balls of radius ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "95150266-35cc-4dc8-b6ea-b3bb3397ba46"],
 " for his measurements. Making constructions and measurements depend on \
scale allows us to study ",
 StyleBox["renormalization of the geometry",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We distinguish several types of substrates for our purposes. ",
 StyleBox["Open substrates",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " represent infinitesimal neighborhoods of their centers, with the observer \
scale set to the radius. As the scale and the graph grow, we have more rays \
from the center, viewed as germs of paths, and we can study the emergence of \
a ",
 StyleBox["linear structure",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " using midpoints, a ",
 StyleBox["Riemannian metric",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " using shortest path projection, and a ",
 StyleBox["Riemann tensor",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " using volume measurements around the center. We can cut out these \
neighborhoods as balls, for example from plane or hyperbolic tessellations, \
and remove rim edges to indicate potential unboundedness."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Following Riemann, we think of space as a ",
 StyleBox["webbing of paths",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": continuous in his case and discrete in ours. He introduced the ",
 StyleBox["Riemannian metric",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " as the simplest infinitesimal quantity producing a length element with \
natural properties. In continuous Riemannian geometry, the metric is extra \
data on a smooth structure. In infrageometry, all geometric information is \
already contained in the graph, and continuous geometry is a property of ",
 StyleBox["Gromov-Hausdorff limits",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " of some sequences of graphs."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Closed substrates",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " represent global objects. Besides path and cycle graphs in one dimension, \
we consider ",
 StyleBox["tessellation graphs of closed surfaces",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " coming from regular and uniform maps. These tessellations should converge \
to continuous Riemannian surfaces, and their combinatorial structure \
determines the genus, Euler characteristic and curvature. We ask whether all \
geometries and geometric objects, such as tangent spaces, canonical forms and \
tensors, can emerge in a ",
 StyleBox["Gromov-Hausdorff limit",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " of metric measure spaces."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Going in the other direction, we discretize continuous geometry using ",
 StyleBox["uniform-length graphs",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", which we construct as contact graphs of hard-sphere packings. These \
graphs capture geometry directly in their combinatorial structure, since no \
edge labels are needed to keep track of lengths. We ask whether such \
discretizations exist for every continuous geometry. We also use ",
 StyleBox["barycentric subdivision",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " to study convergence."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We also consider the ",
 StyleBox["hypergraphs",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " produced by rewriting in the ",
 StyleBox["Wolfram Model",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". These differ from the spatial graphs above. We eventually want to ask \
whether every Riemannian manifold is a ",
 StyleBox["Gromov-Hausdorff limit",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " of a graph sequence generated by a finite collection of rewriting rules, \
and how its geometry is determined by properties of those rules. We think \
that all classical geometry emerges from a binary connectivity relation, so \
we convert hypergraphs to their ",
 StyleBox["1-skeletons",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". The hyperedges themselves could lead to higher geometry."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We also consider ",
 StyleBox["exotic substrates",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " such as trees, complete graphs and fractal graphs. Any substrate can be ",
 StyleBox["inflated",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " by growing a fiber over every vertex; ",
 StyleBox["fibered substrates",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " form a corresponding catalogue of fibrations."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "More concretely, the named example substrates come in ",
 StyleBox["three sizes",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Small\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "993950b8-998c-4577-87b8-4cf81dd90431"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Medium\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "d622f342-c5e6-4948-9d3a-0d894c1ff2e1"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Large\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "08dcc266-93e5-473a-8635-671122820b46"],
 ". ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraSubstrate"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraSubstrate", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["name", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["size", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "0d6afbbb-2986-4a16-820a-a0156d4f772f"],
 " gives the graph, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraSubstrateStyle"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraSubstrateStyle", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["name", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["size", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "b0597dd6-e6ff-4076-86b2-e532264527e4"],
 " gives its drawing options, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraSubstrateCode"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraSubstrateCode", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["name", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["size", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "bc21848c-343a-469d-9fe3-764d576fca2c"],
 " gives the held code that builds it."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

